-          "claim": "At depth $0$ the assertion is the root law. Suppose the increments at depth $k$ are mutually independent with law $N_m(0,(h/2^k)I_m)$. Conditional on these parents, the innovations at their distinct nodes are independent by the input construction; each child pair is the split of step 2.1 with $\\ell=h/2^k$. Thus each pair consists of independent $N_m(0,(h/2^{k+1})I_m)$ vectors. Different pairs are independent because they are measurable functions of disjoint independent parent-innovation pairs. Consequently all depth-$(k+1)$ increments are mutually independent with the claimed law.",
+          "claim": "At depth $0$ the assertion is the root law. Suppose the increments at depth $k$ are mutually independent with law $N_m(0,(h/2^k)I_m)$. The fresh innovations at depth $k$ are mutually independent and independent of the entire parent family by the input construction. Therefore the pairs consisting of each parent and its own innovation are mutually independent across the depth-$k$ nodes. For each such pair, step 2.1 gives the unconditional joint law of its two children: they are independent $N_m(0,(h/2^{k+1})I_m)$ vectors. Since child pairs from distinct parents are measurable functions of disjoint independent parent-innovation pairs, those pairs are mutually independent as well. Hence all depth-$(k+1)$ increments are mutually independent with the claimed law.",
