-          "claim": "Let $\\delta_n=T2^{-n}$, and round each stopping time upward to the next point of this deterministic grid: $\\tau_n=\\delta_n\\lceil\\tau/\\delta_n\\rceil$ and $\\sigma_n=\\delta_n\\lceil\\sigma/\\delta_n\\rceil$. The stopping-time test shows these are finite-valued stopping times, $\\tau_n\\le\\sigma_n$, $\\tau_n\\downarrow\\tau$, and $\\sigma_n\\downarrow\\sigma$. On a grid cell $(t_k,t_{k+1}]$, the process $H^n=\\xi1_{(\\tau_n,\\sigma_n]}$ has coefficient $\\xi1_{\\{\\tau_n\\le t_k<\\sigma_n\\}}$. This coefficient is $\\mathcal F_{t_k}$-measurable: the active event is in $\\mathcal F_{t_k}$, and $\\xi$ restricted to $\\{\\tau_n\\le t_k\\}=\\{\\tau\\le t_k\\}$ is $\\mathcal F_{t_k}$-measurable by [F2]. Thus $H^n$ is a finite predictable step process.",
+          "claim": "First verify that $H_s=\\xi1_{(\\tau,\\sigma]}(s)$ is predictable pointwise. For $s>0$ and Borel $C\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, put $A_C=\\{\\xi\\in C\\}\\in\\mathcal F_\\tau$. Then\n$$A_C\\cap\\{\\tau<s\\}=\\bigcup_{q\\in\\mathbb Q\\cap[0,s)}A_C\\cap\\{\\tau\\le q\\}\\in\\mathcal F_s,$$\nand $\\{\\sigma\\ge s\\}=\\{\\sigma<s\\}^c\\in\\mathcal F_s$, since $\\{\\sigma<s\\}=\\bigcup_{q\\in\\mathbb Q\\cap[0,s)}\\{\\sigma\\le q\\}$. With $E_s=\\{\\tau<s\\}\\cap\\{\\sigma\\ge s\\}$, both $E_s$ and $A_C\\cap E_s$ belong to $\\mathcal F_s$. The preimage $\\{H_s\\in C\\}$ is $A_C\\cap E_s$ if $0\\notin C$, and $(A_C\\cap E_s)\\cup E_s^c$ if $0\\in C$; thus it belongs to $\\mathcal F_s$. Thus $H$ is adapted. Its paths are left-continuous and $H_0=0$. On the nested deterministic grids $t_j^{(r)}=jT2^{-r}$, define $P^{(r)}_0=0$ and $P^{(r)}_s=H_{t_j^{(r)}}$ on each cell $(t_j^{(r)},t_{j+1}^{(r)}]$. Each $P^{(r)}$ is predictable by [F2]; the left endpoints of the cells containing any fixed $s>0$ increase to $s$ from below, so left-continuity gives $P^{(r)}_s\\to H_s$. Hence $H$ is a pointwise limit of predictable processes and is predictable. Now let $\\delta_n=T2^{-n}$, and round each stopping time upward to the next point of this deterministic grid: $\\tau_n=\\delta_n\\lceil\\tau/\\delta_n\\rceil$ and $\\sigma_n=\\delta_n\\lceil\\sigma/\\delta_n\\rceil$. The stopping-time test shows these are finite-valued stopping times, $\\tau_n\\le\\sigma_n$, $\\tau_n\\downarrow\\tau$, and $\\sigma_n\\downarrow\\sigma$. On a grid cell $(t_k,t_{k+1}]$, the process $H^n=\\xi1_{(\\tau_n,\\sigma_n]}$ has coefficient $\\xi1_{\\{\\tau_n\\le t_k<\\sigma_n\\}}$. This coefficient is $\\mathcal F_{t_k}$-measurable: the active event is in $\\mathcal F_{t_k}$, and $\\xi$ restricted to $\\{\\tau_n\\le t_k\\}=\\{\\tau\\le t_k\\}$ is $\\mathcal F_{t_k}$-measurable by [F2]. Thus $H^n$ is a finite predictable step process.",
