-          "claim": "For $R>0$ define $\\rho_R=\\inf\\{t\\le T:|Y_t|\\ge R\\}\\wedge T$, taking $\\rho_R=T$ if the set is empty. For an integer $k\\ge1$, set $\\theta_{R,k}=\\rho_R\\wedge\\tau_k$ and $Y^{R,k}_t=Y_{t\\wedge\\theta_{R,k}}$. Continuity, adaptedness and [F8] make $\\rho_R$ a stopping time, so $\\theta_{R,k}$ is a stopping time. For the first $k$ intervals, multiply each left-endpoint coefficient by $1_{\\{|Y_n|\\le |X_0|\u000bee R\\}}$. These factors are measurable at $\\mathcal F_{\tau_n}$, the truncated coefficients are in $L^2$ by [F5], and they agree with the original coefficients before $\theta_{R,k}$, except at time endpoints which do not affect the integrals. Thus [F3] gives their finite-step integral representation, to which [F2] applies. Before $\\theta_{R,k}$, every active left-endpoint value is bounded by $|X_0|\\vee R$; [F5] and $X_0\\in L^2$ therefore give finite expected coefficient energy. Applying [F2] at $\\theta_{R,k}$ to the finite-step integral representation yields $Y^{R,k}_t=X_0+\\int_0^t1_{[0,\\theta_{R,k}]}(s)\\bar b_s\\,ds+\\int_0^t1_{[0,\\theta_{R,k}]}(s)\\bar\\sigma_s\\,dW_s$, where $\\bar b_s=b(\\tau_n,Y_n)$ and $\\bar\\sigma_s=\\sigma(\\tau_n,Y_n)$ on active intervals. Also $\\sup_{u\\le t}|Y^{R,k}_u|\\le |X_0|\\vee R$, so $U_{R,k}(t):=\\mathbb E\\sup_{u\\le t}|Y^{R,k}_u|^2$ is finite and continuous by [F9], since the path suprema converge pointwise with $t$ and are bounded by the integrable variable $(|X_0|\\vee R)^2$.",
+          "claim": "For $R>0$ define $\\rho_R=\\inf\\{t\\le T:|Y_t|\\ge R\\}\\wedge T$, taking $\\rho_R=T$ if the set is empty. For an integer $k\\ge1$, set $\\theta_{R,k}=\\rho_R\\wedge\\tau_k$ and $Y^{R,k}_t=Y_{t\\wedge\\theta_{R,k}}$. Continuity, adaptedness and [F8] make $\\rho_R$ a stopping time, so $\\theta_{R,k}$ is a stopping time. For the first $k$ intervals, multiply each left-endpoint coefficient by $1_{\\{|Y_n|\\le |X_0|\\vee R\\}}$. These factors are measurable at $\\mathcal F_{\\tau_n}$, the truncated coefficients are in $L^2$ by [F5], and they agree with the original coefficients before $\\theta_{R,k}$, except at time endpoints which do not affect the integrals. Thus [F3] gives their finite-step integral representation, to which [F2] applies. Before $\\theta_{R,k}$, every active left-endpoint value is bounded by $|X_0|\\vee R$; [F5] and $X_0\\in L^2$ therefore give finite expected coefficient energy. Applying [F2] at $\\theta_{R,k}$ to the finite-step integral representation yields $Y^{R,k}_t=X_0+\\int_0^t1_{[0,\\theta_{R,k}]}(s)\\bar b_s\\,ds+\\int_0^t1_{[0,\\theta_{R,k}]}(s)\\bar\\sigma_s\\,dW_s$, where $\\bar b_s=b(\\tau_n,Y_n)$ and $\\bar\\sigma_s=\\sigma(\\tau_n,Y_n)$ on active intervals. Also $\\sup_{u\\le t}|Y^{R,k}_u|\\le |X_0|\\vee R$, so $U_{R,k}(t):=\\mathbb E\\sup_{u\\le t}|Y^{R,k}_u|^2$ is finite and continuous by [F9], since the path suprema converge pointwise with $t$ and are bounded by the integrable variable $(|X_0|\\vee R)^2$.",
