-          "claim": "Apply [F2] to $v=u$ along $\\bar X$. Before $\\tau_X$, the integrand is $\\partial_su+\\mathcal L_Vu=(\\mathcal L_V-\\mathcal G_V)u$ by the backward equation [A3]. After exit, $\\bar X$ is fixed at an exit destination where $u(s,\\bar X_s)=g(\\bar X_s)$ is independent of $s$, so both its time derivative and stopped generator vanish. At time $T$, $u(T,\\bar X_T)=g(\\bar X_T)$, and at time $0$, $u(0,x_0)=\\mathbb E g(\\bar Z_T)$. Therefore $$\\mathbb E g(\\bar X_T)-\\mathbb E g(\\bar Z_T) =\\mathbb E\\int_0^{T\\wedge\\tau_X} (\\mathcal L_V-\\mathcal G_V)u(s,X^V_s)\\,ds.$$",
+          "claim": "Set $\\Psi_V(x)=V(x-x_0)$, $D_V=\\Psi_V(D)$, and let $F_V$ be the images under $\\Psi_V$ of the one-jump exit destinations from $D$. These are disjoint finite subsets of $\\mathbb Z^d$. The transported stopped chain $\\widetilde X=\\Psi_V(\\bar X)$ moves from $y\\in D_V$ by channel increment $\\nu_j$ at rate $Va_j(x_0+y/V)$ and is fixed on $F_V$. For $\\widetilde u(s,y)=u(s,x_0+y/V)$, its time derivative is unchanged and its reaction generator on $D_V$ equals $\\mathcal L_Vu(s,x_0+y/V)$. Apply [F2] to this integer-lattice chain and $\\widetilde u$. Before $\\tau_X$, the integrand is $\\partial_su+\\mathcal L_Vu=(\\mathcal L_V-\\mathcal G_V)u$ by the backward equation [A3]. After exit, $\\bar X$ is fixed at an exit destination where $u(s,\\bar X_s)=g(\\bar X_s)$ is independent of $s$, so both its time derivative and stopped generator vanish. At time $T$, $u(T,\\bar X_T)=g(\\bar X_T)$, and at time $0$, $u(0,x_0)=\\mathbb E g(\\bar Z_T)$. Therefore $$\\mathbb E g(\\bar X_T)-\\mathbb E g(\\bar Z_T) =\\mathbb E\\int_0^{T\\wedge\\tau_X} (\\mathcal L_V-\\mathcal G_V)u(s,X^V_s)\\,ds.$$",
